  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nAn upper-level disturbance passes to our north, but a pressure\ngradient associated with a weak, dry front will swing down\nthrough the area later this afternoon and tonight. Southwest to\nwesterly winds will increase as a result today. Gusts between 35\nand 50 MPH are possible across the mountain ridges tonight,\nwhich has lead to the issuance of a wind advisory for the higher\nelevation zones of Pocahontas and Randolph counties tonight into\nMonday morning. \n\nWinds will shift west-northwest Monday morning as the front \ncrosses. Wind speeds will lessen across the lowlands, but will\nstill be strong across the mountains Monday morning before\ndecreasing in the afternoon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will be in control through Wednesday. Dry weather\nand mostly to partly clear skies are expected through mid week. High\ntemperatures today will be around normal. A warming trend\ncontinues tomorrow and lasts through Wednesday, where portions\nof the lowlands will reach the upper 60s to near 70 degrees by\nTuesday and Wednesday. Drier air paired with these warmer \ntemperatures will create afternoon minimum relative humidity \npercentages in the 30s and 40s today and Monday. Recovery is\nexpected overnight.\n\nClear skies and dry air will force overnight lows to drop into\nthe 30s and 40s for most of the area. upper 20s are possible in\nthe mountain valleys. A frost advisory has been issued tonight\ninto Monday morning for the active growing season counties in\nWest Virginia, Kentucky, and Ohio. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA vigorous, positive-tilt trough ejects into the upper midwest \nand arrives late Wednesday and Thursday. Subsequently, rain \nlikelihood increases from west to east. All rain is expected for\nThursday as the cold front crosses the area, but colder\ntemperatures move in late Thursday into Friday leading to a mix\nof rain/snow or a change over to all snow across the mountains\nThursday night and Friday. \n\nThis solution seems possible, even with not all models on board\njust yet, but the certainty will depend on how a low pressure \ncenter tracks through the area. Some models show a change over \nto snow or mix in portions of the lowlands and foothills Friday \nnight into Saturday due to upslope and northwesterly flow.\nConfidence in accumulations is still low due to uncertainty with\nthe track, but some light accumulations in the mountains may be\npossible Thursday night into Saturday.\n\nNearly all of consensus agrees that colder air, blustery\nnorthwesterly winds, and chances for mountain upslope rain/snow\nshowers will be the trend next weekend.",
